---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import AgentPanel from '../components/AgentPanel.astro';

const title = 'Savoi.ai | Bespoke Software Built Around How Your Business Really Runs';
const description = 'Savoi builds bespoke software by sitting with your team, learning how the business really runs, and turning that into a system you own outright: the code, the harness that runs, tests and deploys it, and the data.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} fileNo="SAVOI-001" classification="FOR PUBLIC RELEASE">
  <!-- ════════ HERO ════════ -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-grid"></div>
    <div class="container hero-inner">
      <div class="hero-content">
        <div class="hero-meta">
          <span class="label hero-meta-item">BESPOKE SOFTWARE // BUILT AROUND HOW YOUR BUSINESS RUNS</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="hero-headline reveal">
          Bespoke Software<br />
          <span class="hero-headline-accent">Built Around How</span><br />
          Your Business Runs
        </h1>
        <p class="hero-subhead reveal">
          Anyone can buy an AI agent off the shelf now, and plenty of them are good, but none of
          them knows how your business actually runs, which is the part that has always made the
          difference. We sit with your team, learn the work the way the people doing it understand
          it, and turn that into a system shaped around your business instead of the other way around.
        </p>
        <p class="hero-category reveal">
          When the build is done you own all of it, the code in your repository, the harness that
          runs, tests and deploys it, and the data in systems you control, so the system belongs to
          your business rather than to us.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions reveal">
          <a href="/contact" class="btn">Scope a Bespoke Build</a>
          <a href="/bespoke" class="btn btn-secondary">How a Bespoke Build Runs</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-bottom-bar">
      <div class="container hero-logos">
        <span class="hero-logos-k label">WORKS INSIDE THE SYSTEMS YOU ALREADY RUN</span>
        <div class="hero-logos-marks">
          <img src="/logos/salesforce.svg" alt="Salesforce" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/oracle.svg" alt="Oracle" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/sap.svg" alt="SAP" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/workday.svg" alt="Workday" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/dynamics-365.svg" alt="Microsoft Dynamics 365" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/netsuite.svg" alt="NetSuite" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/hubspot.svg" alt="HubSpot" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/quickbooks.svg" alt="QuickBooks" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/slack.svg" alt="Slack" width="24" height="24" />
          <img src="/logos/microsoft-teams.svg" alt="Microsoft Teams" width="24" height="24" />
        </div>
        <a href="#integrations" class="hero-logos-link">and the ones nobody else supports →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════ BESPOKE SOFTWARE ════════ -->
  <section class="section paper-texture" id="bespoke-software">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rule-label reveal"><span>§ 01 · Bespoke Software</span></div>
      <h2 class="get-headline reveal">
        We learn how your business runs<br /><span class="get-accent">before we write any code.</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="get-text reveal">
        What makes a bespoke build work is the process rather than any particular tool, because the
        tools are available to everyone now and the understanding of your business is not. We spend
        real time with the people who do the work, we learn where the exceptions live and why the
        spreadsheet sitting next to the ERP exists at all, and that understanding is what ends up in
        the code instead of a vendor's idea of how a business like yours ought to run.
      </p>
      <p class="get-text reveal">
        For a long time, the only alternative to software that almost fit was the old kind of one-off
        software project, where a vendor disappeared for a year with a thick specification,
        came back with something nobody quite recognized, and left you depending on them for every
        change that followed. Those one-off builds earned their reputation for being risky, and most
        businesses learned to live with the workaround instead.
      </p>
      <p class="get-text reveal">
        Bespoke software from Savoi works differently, because it starts with your requirements
        and the way your team actually works, ships a thin working system early so you can use it
        and react to it, and then grows from there in scoped steps you can see. When we are done,
        you own the code, the harness that runs and tests it, and the data it produces, so the
        system belongs to your business rather than to us.
      </p>
      <div class="get-cols">
        <div class="get-col reveal">
          <div class="get-col-k label">HOW A BESPOKE BUILD RUNS</div>
          <ul class="doc-list">
            <li>We start with a two week Diagnostic where we sit with the people who do the work and write the requirements down with you, so everyone agrees on what the system is for before anything gets built</li>
            <li>We ship a thin working system first, small enough to use right away and real enough to learn from</li>
            <li>We extend it in scoped steps, each one agreed in advance, so the build never drifts into a project nobody can see the end of</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="get-col reveal">
          <div class="get-col-k label">WHAT YOU OWN AT THE END</div>
          <ul class="doc-list">
            <li>The code, in your repository</li>
            <li>The harness that runs, tests and deploys it</li>
            <li>The data, in systems you control</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-actions reveal" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <a href="/contact" class="btn">Scope a Bespoke Build</a>
        <a href="/bespoke" class="btn btn-secondary">Read About Bespoke Software</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ════════ EXHIBIT A: TRANSCRIPT ════════ -->
  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rule-label reveal"><span>§ 02 · Proof: What The Process Built For Us</span></div>
      <div class="tx-layout">
        <div class="tx-panel reveal">
          <div class="tx-head label">#finance-ops</div>
          <div class="tx-msg">
            <div class="tx-meta"><span class="tx-who">Finance Lead</span><span class="tx-time">9:41 AM</span></div>
            <p class="tx-text">@Irma new bill. $3,200 from Zoom, due March 15, Operations.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="tx-msg">
            <div class="tx-meta"><span class="tx-who tx-agent">Irma</span><span class="tx-badge">AGENT</span><span class="tx-time">9:41 AM</span></div>
            <p class="tx-text">Created in the ERP as unpaid. Vendor and category match last month, no duplicate found. Approve for the March 14 payment run?</p>
          </div>
          <div class="tx-msg">
            <div class="tx-meta"><span class="tx-who">Finance Lead</span><span class="tx-time">9:43 AM</span></div>
            <p class="tx-text">Approved.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="tx-msg">
            <div class="tx-meta"><span class="tx-who tx-agent">Irma</span><span class="tx-badge">AGENT</span><span class="tx-time">9:43 AM</span></div>
            <p class="tx-text">Queued for payment and logged. It will show in Friday's summary with the other four bills this week.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="tx-side reveal">
          <h2 class="tx-headline">This is what the process builds.</h2>
          <p class="tx-body">
            A bill arrived, got filed correctly, waited for a human yes, and got paid.
            Nobody had to open a portal or upload a PDF to make that happen, and nobody had to remember to reconcile it later, because the system already knew how our books work.
          </p>
          <p class="tx-body">
            That exchange is from our own firm, where Irma has run operations since
            2025. We built her the same way we build for clients, by sitting with the
            work first and writing the system around it, and she is the clearest proof
            we have of what that process produces when it is pointed at a real business.
          </p>
          <a href="/irma" class="btn btn-secondary">Read Irma's File</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════ INTEGRATIONS ════════ -->
  <section class="section paper-texture" id="integrations">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rule-label reveal"><span>§ 03 · What It Plugs Into</span></div>
      <h2 class="int-headline reveal">Built into the systems<br /><span class="int-accent">you already run.</span></h2>
      <p class="int-lede reveal">
        Your ERP and CRM already hold most of the answers, and what they have never had is a system
        that acts on them the way your team does, so a bespoke build does the work inside them: it
        creates the record, drafts the quote, updates the deal, files the bill and builds the report,
        and then brings the handful of decisions that actually need a person to the person who owns them.
      </p>
      <p class="int-lede int-lede-2 reveal">
        Because we learn the business before we write anything, the system ends up knowing your
        products, your customers, your pricing discipline and the exceptions your people handle without
        thinking about it, and it reports back in the tools your team already opens every day rather
        than asking anyone to learn a new portal.
      </p>

      <div class="int-band">
        <div class="int-row int-row-hero reveal">
          <span class="int-row-k label">WORKS INSIDE</span>
          <div class="int-marks int-marks-lg">
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/salesforce.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>Salesforce</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/oracle.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>Oracle</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/sap.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>SAP</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/workday.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>Workday</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/dynamics-365.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>Dynamics 365</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/netsuite.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>NetSuite</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/hubspot.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>HubSpot</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/sage.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>Sage</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/quickbooks.svg" alt="" width="34" height="34" /><span>QuickBooks</span></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="int-row reveal">
          <span class="int-row-k label">AND ACROSS</span>
          <div class="int-marks">
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/microsoft-365.svg" alt="" width="26" height="26" /><span>Microsoft 365</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/google-workspace.svg" alt="" width="26" height="26" /><span>Google Workspace</span></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="int-row reveal">
          <span class="int-row-k label">REPORTS BACK IN</span>
          <div class="int-marks">
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/slack.svg" alt="" width="26" height="26" /><span>Slack</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/microsoft-teams.svg" alt="" width="26" height="26" /><span>Teams</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/imessage.svg" alt="" width="26" height="26" /><span>iMessage</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/telegram.svg" alt="" width="26" height="26" /><span>Telegram</span></span>
            <span class="int-mark"><img src="/logos/whatsapp.svg" alt="" width="26" height="26" /><span>WhatsApp</span></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sys-block">
        <h3 class="sys-headline reveal">Modern SaaS or a system from 1998.<br /><span class="sys-accent">Both are connectable.</span></h3>
        <p class="sys-lede reveal">
          Off-the-shelf AI tools can only reach systems that are already on the internet with a modern
          API, which rules out a lot of what actually runs a mid-market business, and that is one of the
          places a bespoke build earns its keep, because we can run it inside your network and write the
          connection your systems need instead of the one a vendor decided to support.
        </p>

        <div class="sys-cols">
          <div class="sys-col reveal">
            <div class="sys-k label">MODERN SAAS</div>
            <p class="sys-desc">Salesforce, HubSpot, NetSuite, QuickBooks, Microsoft 365, and anything else with a published API.</p>
            <div class="sys-how">Authenticated API access using your own credentials and scoped to what you approve, which is the easy case and usually the first thing we connect.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sys-col reveal">
            <div class="sys-k label">LEGACY AND ON-PREMISE</div>
            <p class="sys-desc">The ERP running on a server in the back room, the AS/400, or the system your vendor stopped updating years ago.</p>
            <div class="sys-how">Direct read access to the database, scheduled exports, watched file drops or the reporting layer it already produces, with no internet exposure required because the system runs inside your network.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="sys-col reveal">
            <div class="sys-k label">HOME-GROWN</div>
            <p class="sys-desc">The Access database somebody built in 2009, the internal tool with one author, or the spreadsheet that is really an application.</p>
            <div class="sys-how">A connector written for your system and yours alone, which is the work off-the-shelf AI will not do and is often where the most valuable work lives.</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="int-custom reveal">
          <div class="int-custom-k label">THE RULE WE WORK BY</div>
          <p class="int-custom-text">
            If a person on your team can get to it, we can usually build the system a way to get to it
            too. Off-the-shelf AI connects to whatever its vendor chose to support and then hands the
            actual work back to you, whereas we write the connection your business runs on and the
            system does the work on the other side of it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="int-note reveal">
        Every connection uses your own credentials, and you decide what it is allowed to change, so
        anything that costs money or leaves the building waits for a human yes. Product names and logos
        belong to their owners and are shown to indicate compatibility, not partnership.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════ WHAT YOU GET ════════ -->
  <section class="section paper-texture">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rule-label reveal">
        <span>§ 04 · What You Actually Get</span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="get-headline reveal">
        A system that knows<br /><span class="get-accent">how your business runs.</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="get-text reveal">
        We sit with your team and learn the work, meaning how you price, how you decide, which
        exceptions matter and what your people actually spend the week doing, and then we build a
        system that does that work inside your own tools and reports back in the channel your team
        already uses. Where an AI agent is the right way to handle part of the work, we build one into
        the system, and where a plain rule or a report does the job better, that is what you get instead.
      </p>
      <p class="get-text reveal">
        It runs the hours your business runs, and anything involving money, a commitment or a customer
        waits for a person, because those rules are written into the requirements before any of it is built.
      </p>

      <div class="get-cols">
        <div class="get-col reveal">
          <div class="get-col-k label">WHAT THE SYSTEM CAN TAKE ON</div>
          <ul class="doc-list">
            <li>Pulling numbers out of the ERP and CRM and turning them into a readable report</li>
            <li>Answering the internal questions that interrupt your senior people all day</li>
            <li>Filing bills, invoices and records into the system correctly the first time</li>
            <li>Chasing the follow-up nobody got to, like stale deals, aging receivables and open tasks</li>
            <li>Drafting the quote, the summary or the recap so it is ready for a person to send</li>
            <li>Watching for the changes that quietly cost money and telling the right person</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="get-col reveal">
          <div class="get-col-k label">WHAT STAYS WITH YOU</div>
          <ul class="doc-list">
            <li>Pricing and any commitment to a customer</li>
            <li>The relationships and the hard conversations</li>
            <li>Judgment calls on exceptions the rules do not cover</li>
            <li>The rules themselves, meaning what the system may do and what it must ask about</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════ HOW IT WORKS ════════ -->
  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rule-label reveal">
        <span>§ 05 · How It Works</span>
      </div>
      <div class="owns-grid">
        <div class="owns-left">
          <h2 class="owns-headline reveal">
            What happens between the first call<br />
            <span class="owns-headline-accent">and a system you own.</span>
          </h2>
          <ul class="owns-list">
            <li class="owns-item reveal">
              <span class="owns-num">01</span>
              <div>
                <h4>It starts from your requirements</h4>
                <p>We write down with your team what the system has to do and what it must never do, so it is shaped by how your business runs rather than by what a product happens to support.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="owns-item reveal">
              <span class="owns-num">02</span>
              <div>
                <h4>It works in your tools</h4>
                <p>Slack, Teams, your CRM, email and your internal systems are where the work happens, so that is where the system does it and where it reports back.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="owns-item reveal">
              <span class="owns-num">03</span>
              <div>
                <h4>Agents go where they earn their place</h4>
                <p>When part of the work needs judgment over messy inputs, we build an agent into the system for that job, and they hand off to each other and to your people so nothing falls through the cracks.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="owns-item reveal">
              <span class="owns-num">04</span>
              <div>
                <h4>You approve anything important</h4>
                <p>Pricing, commitments and big decisions go through a person first, and you set where that line sits.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="owns-item reveal">
              <span class="owns-num">05</span>
              <div>
                <h4>It comes with a harness you own</h4>
                <p>The code that runs, tests and deploys the system lives in your repository alongside it, so any capable team can pick it up, change it and ship it without asking us first.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li class="owns-item reveal">
              <span class="owns-num">06</span>
              <div>
                <h4>Run it yourself, or have us run it</h4>
                <p>Some clients take the system in house once it is built, and others would rather we monitor, maintain and improve it under a managed retainer, the way an MSP runs the network, and either way it stays yours.</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="owns-panel reveal">
          <AgentPanel />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════ WHAT CHANGES ════════ -->
  <section class="section paper-texture">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rule-label reveal">
        <span>§ 06 · What Changes</span>
      </div>
      <div class="changes-grid">
        <div class="changes-col changes-before reveal">
          <div class="changes-header">
            <span class="changes-label label">WITHOUT</span>
            <span class="changes-tag changes-tag-before">BEFORE</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="changes-list">
            <li>Your most experienced person gets interrupted all day for answers that live in a system somewhere</li>
            <li>The weekly report is somebody's Friday afternoon, and it is out of date by Monday</li>
            <li>Receivables get chased when someone remembers</li>
            <li>Deals go quiet and nobody notices until the quarter closes</li>
            <li>The spreadsheet next to the ERP is the real system, and only one person understands it</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="changes-col changes-after reveal">
          <div class="changes-header">
            <span class="changes-label label">WITH A BESPOKE BUILD</span>
            <span class="changes-tag changes-tag-after">AFTER</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="changes-list">
            <li>The system answers from your own data in the channel your team already uses</li>
            <li>The report compiles itself on schedule, from live data</li>
            <li>Aging is reviewed every day and exceptions go to the person who owns them</li>
            <li>Anything left untouched too long gets flagged without anyone having to remember</li>
            <li>The logic that lived in that spreadsheet is written down, tested, and in code you own</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="changes-proof reveal">
        <span class="stamp stamp-green stamp-animate">Case Verified</span>
        <p class="changes-proof-text">
          We have run this process on our own firm and for a global plywood manufacturer, where the trading desk now gets answers from the system instead of interrupting its senior people.
          <a href="/case-studies" class="changes-proof-link">Read the case study →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════ SOVEREIGNTY ════════ -->
  <section class="section section-dark" id="sovereignty-note">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rule-label reveal"><span>§ 07 · Where It Lives</span></div>
      <div class="sov-grid">
        <div class="sov-lead reveal">
          <h2 class="sov-headline">Your secret sauce stays<br /><span class="sov-accent">in the building.</span></h2>
          <p class="sov-text">
            A system built around how your business really runs ends up holding what your company
            actually knows, like which supplier delivers on the promise and which one does not, what a
            particular customer will accept, and what margin to hold and when to walk away. None of
            that is public information, and in our experience it is usually the reason you win business
            your competitors do not.
          </p>
          <p class="sov-text">
            So the important question is where that knowledge lives once it is written down. If it goes
            into a hosted AI platform, the most valuable thing you own becomes a configuration in a
            vendor's account, on infrastructure you do not control, governed by terms you did not write
            and will not see change, and that is exactly the kind of dependence a bespoke build is
            supposed to get you out of.
          </p>
          <p class="sov-text">
            That is why the code sits in your repository, the harness that runs, tests and deploys it
            sits right beside it, and the data stays in systems you control, with every connection using
            your own credentials and read-only by default unless you decide otherwise. Nothing about how
            your company operates ends up as leverage in somebody's renewal conversation, including ours.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="sov-panel reveal">
          <div class="sov-panel-head label">CUSTODY</div>
          <div class="sov-row"><span class="sov-k">Code</span><span class="sov-v">Your repository.</span></div>
          <div class="sov-row"><span class="sov-k">Harness</span><span class="sov-v">Yours. Runs, tests and deploys it.</span></div>
          <div class="sov-row"><span class="sov-k">Data</span><span class="sov-v">In systems you control.</span></div>
          <div class="sov-row"><span class="sov-k">Credentials</span><span class="sov-v">Yours. Read-only by default.</span></div>
          <div class="sov-row"><span class="sov-k">If you fire us</span><span class="sov-v">Everything stays where it is.</span></div>
          <p class="sov-note">
            We treat what your company knows about running itself as an asset, and we think an asset like that belongs with you rather than sitting in somebody else's account where it can come up at renewal time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ════════ FINAL CTA ════════ -->
  <CTA
    headline="Let's scope the system you wish existed."
    subtext="Book a call and walk us through the work it would need to do, and we will tell you plainly whether a bespoke build is the right answer and what the thin first version would look like."
    buttonLabel="Scope a Bespoke Build"
    fileNo="SAVOI-001 / CTA"
  />
</Base>

<style>
  /* HERO */
  .hero {
    background: var(--ink);
    color: var(--paper);
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 4rem 0 0;
  }
  .hero .hero-actions .btn:not(.btn-secondary) {
    background: var(--classification);
    border-color: var(--classification);
    color: var(--paper);
  }
  .hero .hero-actions .btn:not(.btn-secondary):hover {
    background: var(--classification-soft);
    border-color: var(--classification-soft);
    color: var(--paper);
  }
  .hero-grid {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-image:
      repeating-linear-gradient(0deg, transparent, transparent 59px, rgba(244,239,230,0.03) 59px, rgba(244,239,230,0.03) 60px),
      repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, transparent 59px, rgba(244,239,230,0.03) 59px, rgba(244,239,230,0.03) 60px);
    pointer-events: none;
  }
